\date{2025-11-14T19:40:53Z}
\title{Swarm polytypes}

\import{swarm-macros}

\p{Most generally, a Swarm polytype is of the form

##{\swpoly{\overline{\alpha}; \overline{\delta}}{\Delta}{\tau}}

where #{\overline{\alpha}} is a sequence of type variables, #{\overline{\delta}} is a sequence of [capability](001D) set variables, #{\Delta} is a [capability set expression](001Y), and #{\tau} is a monotype.  Such a type is ascribed to expressions which, under any substitutions #{[\overline{\alpha} \mapsto \overline{\tau}]} and #{[\overline{\delta} \mapsto \overline{\Delta}]}, can be evaluated by a robot with [capabilities](001D) #{\Delta}, producing either a value of type #{\tau} or #{\bot} (due to either an exception or nontermination).
}

\p{Note that in the case that there are no capability set variables #{\delta}, we often omit them from the syntax, writing simply \swpoly{\overline{\alpha}}{\Delta}{\tau} as an abbreviation for \swpoly{\overline{\alpha}; \varnothing}{\Delta}{\tau}.  Moreover, we also omit writing #{\Delta \Rightarrow} in the case when #{\Delta} is #{\varnothing}, and omit writing #{\forall} when there are no variables being quantified over.}

